                <pb facs="0582" n="570" xml:id="pg570"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.51/1714: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-02-12T18:00:00-03:00">February 12, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>54. My 45, January 31, 1 p.m. Aranha has observed to me several times recently
                    that one of the chief drawbacks to a solution to the debt controversy is that
                    the British and French have definitely declared that their bondholders council
                    will publicly announce that they are satisfied with the plan of paying 50% of
                    the Aranha plan beginning with the first year of the plan while our bondholders
                    council is unwilling to do anything of the kind in connection with the plan for
                    payments based on the last year of the plan (they also have in mind our
                    council’s remarks about “mock dry” [<hi rendition="#italic">mockery</hi>] last
                    summer and fear possible future repetition) and the Department of State is
                    willing to say only that it is a step in the right direction.</p>
                <p>I have of course explained all the circumstances to Aranha and he is to talk to
                    the President on the subject at Petropolis tomorrow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
